export const meta = {
  name: 'kadai-modules',
  description: 'Kadai business modules: API contracts stage, or build stage (backend + frontend per module in worktrees, then review & fix)',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Contracts', detail: 'module API contract files + cross-module helpers + shared web pickers' },
    { title: 'Build', detail: 'backend and frontend agents per module in the module worktree' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'adversarial review, fixes, missing tests, visual check, commit on mod/<key>' },
  ],
}

// args: { stage: 'contracts' | 'build', group: 'A' | 'B', notes: string (foundation [+ contracts] notes), modules?: string[] }
const NOTES = (args && args.notes) || ''
const GROUP = (args && args.group) || 'A'

const COMMON = (m) => `
You are a senior engineer on the team building "Kadai" (கடை): an enterprise-grade vertical retail ERP for Indian — especially Tamil Nadu — small shops (general stores, supermarkets, textile/apparel stores, pharmacies, footwear, hardware, bakeries). ONE codebase ships as multi-tenant cloud SaaS and as single-tenant on-premise (KADAI_MODE=cloud|onprem). This is production software, not a demo.

WORKTREE: you work ONLY in /home/user/wt/${m.key} (git worktree on branch mod/${m.key}, created from the integration branch after the foundation and all API contracts were committed). node_modules are installed there, and /home/user/wt/${m.key}/.env points DATABASE_URL/DATABASE_SYSTEM_URL at database kadai_dev_${m.key} and TEST_DATABASE_URL/TEST_DATABASE_SYSTEM_URL at kadai_test_${m.key} (both created and migrated). Never modify /home/user/my_shop (the integration checkout) or any other worktree under /home/user/wt/.
Other agents work concurrently: inside this same worktree your module counterpart(s) (backend/frontend) work on other paths; other modules are being built in other worktrees.
If you run dev servers use ports: API ${m.port}, web dev ${m.port + 100}; run them in the background and kill them before finishing.

READ FIRST (binding contracts): docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/API.md (your module's section + anything you call), docs/PRODUCT.md, apps/server/migrations/*.sql (the tables you touch), packages/shared/src/api/common.ts and packages/shared/src/api/${m.contract}.ts (your module's API contract), plus the shared domain helpers you need (pricing.ts, gst.ts, units.ts, money.ts, fy.ts, permissions.ts, features.ts, settings.ts, verticals.ts, enums.ts, errors.ts). Follow the conventions exactly: snake_case JSON, *_paise integers, *_milli quantities, *_bp rates, pack pricing, envelopes {data}/{data,page}, error codes, permission + feature gating, req.tx() tenant transactions, core posting services as the only writers of stock/journal/payments/numbering.

Foundation & contracts notes from the agents who built them (APIs you must use):
${NOTES}

RESUMING: the machine running these agents restarts occasionally and kills running agents; files on disk survive. FIRST run git -C /home/user/wt/${m.key} status, git diff --stat and git log --oneline claude/laughing-hamilton-cleqkb..HEAD (earlier attempts may have saved WIP commits), and look at your owned paths: if a previous attempt of your role already wrote work there, read it and CONTINUE from it (verify, complete, fix) — do not start over or throw it away. If PostgreSQL/Redis are not reachable, start them: pg_ctlcluster 16 main start; redis-server --daemonize yes.

Hard rules:
- You own ONLY the paths listed in your task. Small, clearly necessary fixes elsewhere are allowed only if your module cannot work otherwise; list every such change in your report ("cross-module changes"). Never refactor other modules.
- Contract file packages/shared/src/api/${m.contract}.ts: you may make corrective/additive changes needed by your module. Record every change in a "// CONTRACT CHANGELOG" comment block at the top of that file (one line each) so the counterpart agent sees it. Never change other modules' contract files (report needed changes instead).
- New migrations only inside your band ${m.band}; after adding one run: pnpm db:migrate (dev DB) and pnpm db:types, and the test DB is migrated by the test global setup.
- New npm dependencies: avoid; if unavoidable use: flock /tmp/kadai-pnpm.lock pnpm --filter <pkg> add <dep> (inside the worktree) and report it.
- Do NOT git commit (the review agent commits for the module) unless your task says so.
- Strict TypeScript, no 'any' (except narrow library boundaries with a comment), no TODO/placeholder stubs in your scope, no skipped/disabled tests. Real validation, error handling and permission checks on every route.
- All user-visible strings via i18n with BOTH Tamil (natural, polite, shopkeeper-friendly Tamil — not machine-literal) and English in apps/web/src/locales/{ta,en}/<namespace>.json for your namespaces.
- Before finishing, run and make green: pnpm --filter @kadai/shared typecheck, pnpm --filter @kadai/server typecheck, pnpm --filter @kadai/web typecheck (as relevant to your side), the tests of your module, and eslint on the files you touched (pnpm exec eslint <paths>). Report exact commands and results.
`

const REPORT = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    summary: { type: 'string' },
    files: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    verification: { type: 'string' },
    contract_changes: { type: 'string', description: 'changes made to the module contract file' },
    cross_module_changes: { type: 'string', description: 'any change outside owned paths, and needed changes in other modules' },
    known_gaps: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['summary', 'files', 'verification', 'contract_changes', 'cross_module_changes', 'known_gaps'],
}

const BACKEND = (m) => `${COMMON(m)}
YOUR ROLE: BACKEND for the "${m.title}" module.
You own: ${m.serverPaths}; packages/shared/src/api/${m.contract}.ts (contract adjustments as described); migrations band ${m.band}.

Implement every endpoint of the "${m.apiSection}" section(s) of docs/API.md that belongs to this module. Register each one FROM the route catalogue in packages/shared/src/api/${m.contract}.ts with the server's registerRoute/registerRoutes helper (apps/server/src/core) so method, path, schemas and permission/feature/mode guards come from the contract (add a route entry to the contract if one is genuinely missing, and log it in the CONTRACT CHANGELOG), with permission (and feature/plan) gating, store scoping (ctx.storeIds) where data is store-specific, pagination/filtering/sorting/CSV where specified, audit entries for sensitive actions, and idempotency for financial creates.

Module specification:
${m.be}

Tests (Vitest integration via app.inject against the module test DB, using createTestTenant and helpers from apps/server/src/test): cover every endpoint's happy path, validation errors, permission denial (use a cashier/limited role), feature gating (tenant on 'free' plan where the feature is not included), tenant isolation (data from tenant B never visible to tenant A), and the module's domain invariants listed above. Place them under apps/server/src/modules/${m.key}/__tests__/.
Also register your jobs (if any) in modules/${m.key}/jobs.ts and test the handlers directly.
`

const FRONTEND = (m, fe) => `${COMMON(m)}
YOUR ROLE: FRONTEND${fe.label ? ' (' + fe.label + ')' : ''} for the "${m.title}" module.
You own: ${fe.paths}; i18n namespace files ${fe.namespaces.map((n) => 'apps/web/src/locales/{ta,en}/' + n + '.json').join(', ')}.
The backend for this module is being implemented concurrently in this same worktree against the same contract (packages/shared/src/api/${m.contract}.ts). Build strictly against the contract and call every endpoint through the typed client (callRoute / useRouteQuery / useRouteMutation in apps/web/src/lib) with the contract's route catalogue entries — never hand-written URL strings; before finishing, re-read the contract file (check its CONTRACT CHANGELOG block) and adapt. Use MSW handlers (fixtures shaped exactly like the contract) for component/flow tests. Endpoints of OTHER modules you call must be used exactly as their contract files in packages/shared/src/api/ define them (they are committed); reuse the shared pickers/hooks in apps/web/src/components (built in the contracts phase) instead of re-implementing them.

UX bar: this is a commercial product for busy Tamil shopkeepers — fast keyboard flows, large tap targets, clear Tamil copy, Indian formats (₹1,23,456.00, dd-MM-yyyy), sensible defaults, empty states that teach, skeleton loading, optimistic feedback with toasts, confirm dialogs for destructive actions, permission-aware UI (hide/disable what the user can't do via can()/hasFeature()), responsive down to 390px phones (tables → cards), no layout jank, accessible labels/focus.
Replace the placeholder pages of your routes (apps/web/src/features/<name>/routes.tsx) with real pages, keeping the route paths unchanged.

Screens & behaviour to build:
${fe.spec}

Tests: Vitest + Testing Library + MSW for the critical flows of your screens (forms validation & submit payloads matching the contract, list filtering/pagination calls, permission-hidden actions). Visual self-check: build and run the web (vite dev on port ${m.port + 100} with the API at ${m.port} if the backend is runnable, or MSW in the browser via a dev-only mock mode if not) and take Playwright screenshots (chromium preinstalled at /opt/pw-browsers; write any throwaway script under /tmp, not in the repo) of your main pages in Tamil and English at 1366x768 and 390x844 into /tmp/kadai-shots/${m.key}/; open them with the Read tool and fix visual problems.
`

const REVIEW = (m, reports) => `${COMMON(m)}
YOUR ROLE: REVIEWER & FINISHER for the "${m.title}" module (you may edit any path owned by this module: ${m.serverPaths}; ${m.frontends.map((f) => f.paths).join('; ')}; the module contract file; band ${m.band} migrations; module i18n files).

Reports from the build agents:
${reports}

Do, in order:
1) Run everything: pnpm install check (flock), pnpm --filter @kadai/shared typecheck/test, pnpm --filter @kadai/server typecheck, server tests of this module (and the full server suite once), pnpm --filter @kadai/web typecheck/test/build, eslint for touched paths. Fix all failures at the root cause.
2) Contract alignment: every frontend call matches the backend route + schema exactly (paths, methods, query params, field names, envelopes, error codes). Diff the contract's CONTRACT CHANGELOG against both sides.
3) Adversarial review against the module specification below and ARCHITECTURE.md — look for: wrong GST/money maths or rounding, posting that bypasses core services, missing transaction boundaries, missing row locks on documents being posted/cancelled, race conditions, missing permission/feature/store-scope checks, cross-tenant leaks (raw systemDb use), unvalidated input, N+1 queries or missing indexes for list filters, broken pagination totals, idempotency gaps, missing audit entries, incorrect status transitions (e.g. cancel twice), i18n gaps (hard-coded strings, missing ta keys — compare ta vs en key sets), poor UX/accessibility, missing empty/loading/error states. Fix every real issue; add regression tests.
4) Run the backend + frontend together (API port ${m.port}, web ${m.port + 100}) against the module dev DB, create realistic data for the relevant vertical(s) (Tamil names), exercise the main flows through the browser with a Playwright script under /tmp, take screenshots into /tmp/kadai-shots/${m.key}/review-*.png, Read them, and fix problems. Kill servers afterwards.
5) Commit all module work on branch mod/${m.key} in the worktree (git add -A; git commit) with a descriptive message ending with these two lines exactly:
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01R9SwozABHzZsWcbEK6M9h1
Do not push. Do not merge.

Module specification (for reference):
BACKEND:
${m.be}
FRONTEND:
${m.frontends.map((f) => (f.label ? '[' + f.label + '] ' : '') + f.spec).join('\n')}
`

